    {
      "id": "doordash.screen.shopping_item",
      "priority": 75,
      "comment": "#920 — the item-details card renders the customer's own free-text note TWICE: a fused `contentDescription` on the container ('Customer Notes: <note>') and the value in the `body_text_view` child, with a `label_text_view` sibling reading exactly 'Customer Notes'. It is customer-AUTHORED free text (the fielded 07-29 note was benign — 'Yellow bunch:' — but the field is the #803 class: it can carry a gate code, an apartment, a name), and this rule declared no `redact` at all, so it shipped verbatim on a RECOGNIZED surface. Both entries take `plainMask` (#795) rather than the `<4hex>` distinctness suffix: a note has an UNBOUNDED alphabet but no lower bound on LENGTH, so a short note that IS the secret ('4821', 'Gate 12') sits above #889's 4-char floor while its plaintext space stays far below the suffix's 65 536 buckets — exactly the small-space case plainMask exists for. Distinctness buys nothing here either: unlike a customer name, a note is not a join key and feeds no lineage. `label_text_view` ('Customer Notes') is app chrome and stays raw so the masked card still reads as a note on replay; the item name, price, PLU, aisle and store-instruction nodes are merchant/driver-owned and are untouched. Scope check: 'Customer Notes' appears on no other doordash surface across the committed corpus or the 07-30/08-09 pulls — the sibling shopping rules (`shopping_checkout`/`shopping_item_status`/`shopping_wrong_item`, verify-items) never field it — so this is the one owner today; a later sibling that starts rendering it needs its own entry.",
      "redact": [
        {
          "comment": "The container's fused contentDescription ('Customer Notes: <note>'). Matched by desc (the node has no id and no text), keepPrefix keeps the label half.",
          "find": {
            "hasDescContaining": "Customer Notes: "
          },
          "keepPrefix": [
            "Customer Notes: "
          ],
          "plainMask": true
        },
        {
          "comment": "The note VALUE node. `body_text_view` is a GENERIC LEGO-card id (the corpus carries it on pickup_arrival too), so it is conjoined with #860's label-sibling predicate — 'Customer Notes' is the immediately preceding sibling — which anchors on app chrome vocabulary rather than on the note's own shapeless text.",
          "find": {
            "all": [
              {
                "hasIdSuffix": "body_text_view"
              },
              {
                "hasPrecedingSiblingText": "Customer Notes"
              }
            ]
          },
          "plainMask": true
        }
      ],
      "state": {
        "flow": "task:pickup:arrived"
      },
      "require": {
        "exists": {
          "hasIdSuffix": "fragmentContainerView_shopDeliver"
        }
      },
      "reject": [
        {
          "exists": {
            "hasText": "Shop and Deliver"
          }
        }
      ]
    },
